  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Daytime heating has brought MLCAPE values of \n1000-2000 J/kg to much of the region, with further destabilization\nexpected as we get later into the afternoon. A stubborn dry \nlayer above 700mb continues along and south of 70 with slightly \nmore moist profiles north of hwy 70. As a shortwave sits \noverhead, scattered to numerous showers are occuring north of \nhwy 70 where there is more moisture to play with, decreasing in \ncoverage and intensity south with the drier air aloft. \nMeanwhile, sea breeze moves through today. The interaction \nbetween convection to the north and sea breeze could allow \nshowers and tstorms to migrate south closer to hwy 70 in the \nlate afternoon/early evening hours, but expect this convection \nto be more transient and tame in comparison. \n\nOverall, convective threats for today are localized flash\nflooding where heavy rain rates persist over already saturated\nsoils, and gust winds 30-50mph as DCAPE values near 1000 J/kg\nresult in some downbursts. Lack of robust updraft development\nprecludes any meaningful severe threat today. \n\nAs we approach sunset, any lingering convection should quickly\ntaper off with a loss of daytime heating and the shortwave\nmoving offshore. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An anomalously strong upper level trough is \nforecast to develop over the Mid-Atlantic Region on Tuesday, \nwith a potent shortwave diving southeast through Virginia and \nthe Carolinas Tuesday into Tuesday night. A strong LLJ ahead of \nthis feature should lead to increasing moisture advection, with \nPWATs once again climbing back above 2\". Meanwhile, heating of a\nmoist boundary layer should support moderate instability, with \nMLCAPE rising into the 1000-1500j/kg range. Aloft, a notable \nupper jet max is forecast to overspread the instability axis, \nsupporting deep layer shear of 30-45kt. While instability will \nbe moderate, the shear/instability combo is notable for this \ntime of year. \n\nSynoptically, the combination of all of the factors above \nchecks several boxes for severe weather potential. This also \nhas modest support from ensemble, analog, and machine learning \nguidance. With the 12Z model suite, some uncertainty has\npresented itself regarding tstorm coverage. The main potential \nlimiting factors are timing and potential subsidence. Some \nguidance suggests convection may not reach ENC until after \nsunset. Despite the potential for a gradually stabilizing \nboundary layer, if strong large-scale forcing is realized, it \nshould help support deep convection well past the typical \ndiurnal max in convective intensity this time of year. Given the\npotential amount of forcing present, it appears likely that an \nMCS will move through the eastern Carolinas and VA during the \nevening and early overnight hours posing a risk of damaging \nwinds. Low- level SRH around 100-200m2/s2 may support a few \nrotating updrafts capable of hail and tornados. However, those \nlatter 2 hazards should be lower compared to the wind risk. \nLastly, while a MCS would be more progressive, the high PWAT \nairmass should support heavy rainfall rates which may support a \nshort-fused flooding risk, especially for the area hardest hit \nthe past few days (ie. Hyde County east through the Outer \nBanks).\n\nAs alluded to earlier, with the most recent CAMs an drier \noutcome has presented itself as a possibility. With the low \nsetting up shop in the northeast, it is possible the best \nforcing shifts to the north with the low and drier air sets up \nabove 700mb suppressing convection. The 12Z RRFS, and FV3 are \ntwo of the models that suggest this outcome. While most guidance\nis still showing robust convection moving through the region, \nthe introduction of this possibility decreases confidence on \ncoverage of strong to severe storms tomorrow.\n\nAhead of the convection, hot and humid conditions are expected,\nwith heat indices rising to 100-105 degrees. This appears to be\na bit marginal for heat headlines, but is something we'll \ncontinue to assess in later forecasts.",
